# CODEOWNERS — https://docs.github.com/repositories/managing-your-repositories-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners
#
# TruthLens X is a single-maintainer repository today, so every path resolves to the owner.
# The file exists anyway, for two reasons that are not about headcount:
#
#   1. It makes review *required* on the paths where a silent change is expensive. Once branch
#      protection requires review from code owners, the paths below cannot be merged by an
#      automated or unreviewed change — which matters most for the gates that police licensing
#      and evidence integrity, because those are exactly the files a well-meaning "quick fix"
#      tends to relax first.
#   2. It documents intent. When a second maintainer arrives, this file is the map of which
#      areas need a specialist, and the comments say why.
#
# Syntax note: later patterns take precedence over earlier ones, so the specific rules are
# listed after the catch-all.

# ── Catch-all ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*                                   @MuhammadAhmad7227

# ── Decisions and architecture ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An ADR is a record of a decision that was ratified against alternatives; editing one is
# changing a decision, not editing prose. docs/DECISIONS.md is the register that the ADRs,
# the roadmap (§ "Decision traceability") and the security gates all cite by number, so a
# renumbering here breaks references in code comments and CI messages across the repository.
/docs/adr/                          @MuhammadAhmad7227
/docs/DECISIONS.md                  @MuhammadAhmad7227
/docs/SYSTEM_ARCHITECTURE.md        @MuhammadAhmad7227
/docs/SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE.md      @MuhammadAhmad7227

# ── Licensing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADR-0008: the repository is deliberately UNLICENSED until the owner records a decision.
# There is no LICENSE file, and that absence is asserted by a CI gate rather than left to
# chance — see "No implied licence grant in package metadata" in .github/workflows/security.yml.
# The audit below tracks every third-party licence and the legal gates (L-1 … L-11) that must
# clear before a dependency may be added, so it is owned as carefully as the ADRs.
/docs/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSE_AUDIT.md  @MuhammadAhmad7227

# ── Security gates and CI ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These files are the enforcement layer. A change here can silently disable a check while
# leaving the workflow green, which is worse than a failing build: it removes the signal and
# keeps the confidence. Every gate in security.yml has a negative control for this reason.
/.github/workflows/security.yml     @MuhammadAhmad7227
/.github/                           @MuhammadAhmad7227

# ── Evidence integrity and configuration ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Anything that touches hashing, custody, retention or the production configuration refusals
# (DEBUG/TESTING off, EVIDENCE_ENCRYPTION != disabled, STORAGE_BACKEND != filesystem, TZ == UTC,
# ALPR_ALLOW_REGISTRY_LOOKUP refused in every environment per ADR-0013).
/backend/app/core/                  @MuhammadAhmad7227
/backend/tests/security/            @MuhammadAhmad7227
/backend/tests/architecture/        @MuhammadAhmad7227

# ── Deployment ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Single-host Docker Compose (ADR-0005). Only nginx may publish ports; the API and Postgres
# stay on the internal 172.28.0.0/24 network, which is also the pinned value of
# FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS in api.Dockerfile. Widening that to `*` would let a client spoof
# X-Forwarded-For into the audit log.
/infra/                             @MuhammadAhmad7227

# ── The AI engine contract ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ai_services must stay free of third-party runtime dependencies and must never import the
# backend (SYSTEM_ARCHITECTURE §4.1 rule 5). Engines arrive as plugins through the
# `truthlens.engines` entry-point group, so a dependency added here means the contract itself
# started reaching out to a third party — see ADR-0012 and ADR-0021.
/ai_services/                       @MuhammadAhmad7227
